    "title": "Temperatures flip from feeling like late February to mid-may within 24 hours - NewsBreak",
    "text": "By Bill Snyder BJ Lutz Demetrius Ivory WGN-TV ,\n1 days ago\n*NOTE: This article is now an almanac of Wednesday’s weather. We have published a new forecast story for Thursday and beyond .\nWhat a difference a day makes! Wednesday high temperatures soared into the low 70s following a chilly start to the day.\nThe official high temperature of 71 degrees at both airports makes it eight times in 2026 that the mercury has reached or exceeded that mild benchmark. Only three other years since official records began in 1871 have seen as many or more 70s by now: 13 in 2012; 10 in 1945; and 8 in 1963.\nThe warmth was aided by the day’s 85% possible sunshine and powerhouse southerly winds which gusted past 40 mph at times.\nWednesday Chicago area temperatures How much warmer than the same time on Tuesday afternoon? Late afternoon Midwest temperatures The Chicago area is not alone; the significant warmup was prevalent across many other Midwestern cities.\nHow much warmer than same time on Tuesday? Wednesday U.S. highs & departure from normal More than 163 million U.S. residents enjoyed a real springlike day at or above 70 degrees with more than 46 million at or above 80 degrees. Highs across the Midwest were generally 5-15 degrees above normal. Chilly across the Northeast and Mid-Atlantic with highs about 10 degrees below normal.\nWednesday allergy report Tree pollen dropped slightly in the latest report from Loyola Medicine. Mold remains low.\nClimate and Environment news: WGN Weather Center blog\nCopyright 2026 Nexstar Media, Inc. All rights reserved. This material may not be published, broadcast, rewritten, or redistributed. For the latest news, weather, sports, and streaming video, head to WGN-TV.\n2",
    "summary": "By Bill Snyder BJ Lutz Demetrius Ivory WGN-TV , *NOTE: This article is now an almanac of Wednesday’s weather. The official high temperature of 71 degrees at both airports makes it eight times in 2026 that the mercury has reached or exceeded that mild benchmark. The warmth was aided by the day’s 85% possible sunshine and powerhouse southerly winds which gusted past 40 mph at times. Late afternoon Midwest temperatures The Chicago area is not alone; the significant warmup was prevalent across many other Midwestern cities.",
